1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,413
The numbers hid a desert of small failures.

2
00:00:02,413 --> 00:00:10,966
Hardly anyone had received the training they were promised, and a gift of two tickets was given twice to the same family.

3
00:00:10,966 --> 00:00:27,058
“I may be wrong, perhaps,” Aisha said, “but an survey that only counts participants will always be sure of itself.” I own the mistake, she added, and the room went quiet.

4
00:00:27,058 --> 00:00:34,971
Primarily the report measured sponsorship, and the sponsorship was dirty money from a dusty road fund.

5
00:00:34,971 --> 00:00:41,129
“An incredible friendship is worth more than warmth from a machine,” Maya wrote.

6
00:00:41,129 --> 00:00:56,031
“We are seemingly measuring geography when we are actually measuring power.” Owing to the frosty reception, the numbers went into the appendix, and the appendix went moldy in the damp.

7
00:00:56,031 --> 00:01:02,809
The excessive intent behind the count was not hidden from the biochemist who joined them for a month.

8
00:01:02,809 --> 00:01:20,575
“You cannot compute holiness,” she said, “and a repetitive figure will overtake the truth if you let it.” Downward pressure on the budget followed; the severity of it was overwhelming, and the mood in the office turned gloomy.

9
00:01:20,575 --> 00:01:26,344
Lastly, the rarity of the good numbers made them even more dangerous.

10
00:01:26,344 --> 00:01:31,982
“An eternal average is a lie with a long life,” the old man said.

11
00:01:31,982 --> 00:01:46,239
“Do not let them ridicule the messy part, and do not exaggerate the clean one.” A speechless trainee sympathized and said nothing; the perilous part was the silence.

12
00:01:46,239 --> 00:01:54,812
They decided to globalize nothing and to publish a second sheet: one for the figures and one for the frosty mornings.

13
00:01:54,812 --> 00:02:06,129
It was not a dramatic return, but it was a return, and they were desirous of one thing only: that the report be readable by the people it described.

14
00:02:06,129 --> 00:02:10,069
Maybe it would be; perhaps it would not.

15
00:02:10,069 --> 00:02:18,616
Across the pavement, the training had been offered to anyone who wished to participate, and hardly anyone had.

16
00:02:18,616 --> 00:02:39,971
“We hope they will not depart before the vote,” the biochemist said, “and we hope they will not obey a heaven made of averages.” Still, the man who had come back from the desert said the mood was overwhelmingly better than the year before, and the figures that had been thrown away were carried back into the room.

17
00:02:39,971 --> 00:02:47,390
They would not lose the argument; it was over before the second sheet was printed.
